      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right to be concerned about that mess, but don't worry, because the rules for 8 authors are actually quite straightforward once you get the hang of it. In **APA 7th**, you simply list all eight authors' names in the first citation, separated by commas, and then use an ampersand before the last name. For example: (Smith, Jones, Doe, Brown, Green, White, Black, & Grey, 2023). After that, if you cite them again in the same paper, you just use \"et al.\" (meaning \"and others\") to keep things tidy.\n\nIf you prefer **Chicago style**, the approach is a bit more flexible depending on whether you are using the notes-bibliography or author-date system. In the author-date version, it's similar to APA: list all eight names the first time, then switch to \"et al.\" for subsequent citations. However, if you are using the notes-bibliography system, you would list all eight authors in the footnotes or endnotes the first time they appear, and then shorten them to \"Smith et al.\" in later notes.\n\nTo avoid the visual clutter of eight names, remember that the key",
