    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, there is a distinct difference in how the first mention works versus subsequent ones for Chicago, and it's a bit like a two-stage process for the paper's structure.\n\nIn the **Notes-Bibliography** system (the most common for humanities), the first time you mention an 8-author paper, you list all eight names in the footnote or endnote, separated by commas, with an ampersand before the last one. For example: *Smith, Jones, Doe, Brown, Green, White, Black, & Grey*. However, for the *second* citation of that same paper, you drop the names and just write \"Smith et al.\" in the note, followed by the page number.\n\nIf you are using the **Author-Date** system instead, the rule is actually much simpler and more consistent. You list all eight authors the first time you cite them in the text, just like in APA, and then switch to \"Smith et al.\" for every single subsequent citation. There is no special \"first mention\" rule that changes the format later; it's just all-or-nothing based on whether it's the first time or not.\n\nTo avoid the mess, just remember that Chicago's Notes-B",
